# Wildframe local development stack -- example environment file
#
# Copy to `deployments/.env` and edit. `deployments/.env` is git-ignored; this
# example is committed. Docker Compose reads this file automatically because it
# sits next to the compose file it is used with:
#
#     cp deployments/.env.example deployments/.env
#     docker compose -f deployments/docker-compose.dev.yml up -d
#
# Every value below is optional. Each one has a working development default
# baked into the compose file (the VAR-default form) or into the service's
# settings module, so the stack starts with no .env at all. Set a value here
# only when you want to override that default.
#
# SECURITY: this file is for LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY. Never put a live-mode
# Stripe secret key here, and never commit a real credential to any tracked
# file. The Stripe placeholders below are intentionally non-functional: Stripe
# rejects them, so they cannot authenticate against any real account. See
# SECURITY.md.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe (billing-service)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional. Without these, billing-service still boots and /health still
# reports healthy, but the checkout routes (POST /api/v1/billing/subscribe for
# the SVOD tier, and POST /api/v1/billing/purchase for TVOD) return
# 502 "Payment provider unavailable" because the placeholder key is rejected.
# That 502 means "Stripe is not configured locally", not "Stripe is down".
#
# To exercise real Stripe behaviour, create a free test-mode account at
# https://dashboard.stripe.com/test/apikeys and paste the *test* keys here.
# Test keys are safe to use locally; they cannot move real money.
#
# STRIPE_API_KEY: the secret key from Dashboard -> Developers -> API keys.
STRIPE_API_KEY=sk_test_default_change_me

# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET: from Dashboard -> Webhooks -> your endpoint -> Signing
# secret. Leave the default to replay the local webhook tests; those sign with
# this exact value (tests/integration/conftest.py), so changing it here without
# updating that suite breaks webhook signature verification.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_default_change_me

# STRIPE_SVOD_PRICE_ID: a recurring Price ID from Dashboard -> Products, used
# for the SVOD checkout session. The placeholder is not a real Stripe object.
STRIPE_SVOD_PRICE_ID=price_default_svod

# STRIPE_SUCCESS_URL / STRIPE_CANCEL_URL: where Stripe redirects the browser
# after checkout. Point these at your local frontend when testing a real
# checkout round-trip (for example http://localhost:3000/billing/success).
STRIPE_SUCCESS_URL=https://wildframe.com/billing/success
STRIPE_CANCEL_URL=https://wildframe.com/billing/cancel


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kafka client credentials (all backend services)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These have NO default in the compose file: each is interpolated as
# ${SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD} with no fallback, so an unset value resolves to an
# empty string and the service fails SASL PLAIN authentication against the
# broker. Nothing in the repository generates them today, and CI never starts
# this stack, so a fresh clone that runs `docker compose up` without setting
# these will see the services fail to authenticate to Kafka.
#
# They are read as plain environment variables, so generate them once and keep
# them in your local .env:
#
#     for svc in AUTH USER CONTENT STREAMING SEARCH RECOMMENDATION BILLING \
#                ANALYTICS NOTIFICATION CREATORS MODERATION UPLOADS \
#                MEDIA_PIPELINE ADMIN; do
#       echo "${svc}_KAFKA_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -hex 24)"
#     done
#
# ...then paste the output below. The broker's JAAS section in the compose file
# references each of these names, so all fifteen are required together.
#
# The *_KAFKA_USERNAME values already default to the service name and rarely
# need setting.
#
# AUTH_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# USER_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# CONTENT_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# STREAMING_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# SEARCH_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# RECOMMENDATION_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# BILLING_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# ANALYTICS_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# NOTIFICATION_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# CREATORS_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# MODERATION_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# UPLOADS_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# MEDIA_PIPELINE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# ADMIN_SERVICE_KAFKA_PASSWORD=
# API_GATEWAY_KAFKA_PASSWORD=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TLS (Kafka inter-broker)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These already default to the shared development value wildframe-dev, which is
# what scripts/generate-dev-certs.sh writes. Override only if you regenerate
# the development keystore with your own passwords.
#
# KAFKA_SSL_KEYSTORE_PASSWORD=wildframe-dev
# KAFKA_SSL_TRUSTSTORE_PASSWORD=wildframe-dev
# KAFKA_SSL_KEY_PASSWORD=wildframe-dev
# KAFKA_INTER_BROKER_USER=kafka
# KAFKA_INTER_BROKER_PASSWORD=wildframe-dev
# KAFKA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=wildframe-dev
